 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to the imposition of an occupancy tax\nin the city of Cortland; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nthe expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7778",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7778-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §1202-gg, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7778\n\nSPONSOR: SEWARD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the tax law, in relation to the imposition of an occu-\npancy tax in the city of Cortland; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo authorize the City of Cortland to enact, at local option, a room\noccupancy tax on transient room rentals.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the tax law by adding a new section, 1202-ff, (1)\nproviding authorization for the City of Cortland to impose a tax of up\nto three percent on hotel and motel room rentals; (2) designates\nofficial(s) for the collection and administration of said tax, person(s)\nliable for payment, recipients and method of collection; (3) provides an\nexemption from taxation to government entities, religious, charitable\nand educational organizations and organizations for the prevention of\ncruelty to children or animals; (4) provides for a process of review in\naccordance with civil practice law and rules; and (5) designates that\nrevenues from such tax be paid to the treasury of city of Cortland to be\ncredited to and deposited into a city hospitality tax fund.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date with a sunset of September 1,\n2023.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to City of Cortland Common Council resolution  21 of 2020, this\nbill would grant the City of Cortland the home rule authority to enact a\n\"bed tax\" on hotel and motel room rentals. The receipts will be deposit-\ned in a \"hospitality tax fund\" for water, sewer and storm water infras-\ntructure repairs, the construction of pedestrian pathways and bicycle\nways to link motels to recreational and commercial facilities catering\nto travelers, improve streets' visual appeal, and support events and\nattractions that promote the city and region as a destination. ò\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A7852-B, advanced to 3' reading calendar.\n\n2019: A7390, Passed Assembly & Senate, Vetoed memo. 250\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State of New York.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate and shall expire and be deemed repealed on September 1, 2023.\nThe city will be required to enact its own local law.\n",
